      "example_answers": [
        "Introduce a \"blitz clock\" that randomly activates mid-game, forcing players to make moves within 5 seconds for a short burst, adding tension and potential blunders. Additionally, add a \"wildcard\" rule where once per game each player can invoke a \"gambit token\" to secretly swap one of their captured pieces back onto the board in a legal position, creating dramatic surprise moments. Display both players' remaining thinking time prominently, play subtle tension music as time runs low, and use a large digital board with color highlights showing threatened pieces and possible moves so spectators can easily follow the action and anticipate drama.",
        "Introduce a \"bounty square\" system where certain squares on the board are randomly designated before the game as high-value zones \u2014 any piece captured on a bounty square scores bonus points. This encourages aggressive play toward those squares, creating natural focal points for spectators to watch. Combine this with a \"challenge\" rule where each player can once per game declare a \"duel\" on a specific piece, meaning if that piece is captured, the capturing player wins a point advantage. This gives spectators a narrative thread to follow \u2014 watching whether the challenged piece survives. Finally, add a visible \"aggression meter\" tracking how many captures and checks have occurred, and if the game goes too long without action, a neutral \"pressure rule\" kicks in that removes one pawn from each side, gradually forcing confrontation and preventing drawn-out boring endgames.",
        "Add a \"power square\" mechanic where one randomly revealed square per game grants any piece occupying it a one-time ability to move like a queen for a single turn. This creates a strategic race and visible tension around one location on the board. Pair this with a \"sudden death zone\" rule in long games: after move 40, if no capture has occurred in 10 moves, a random pawn on each side is automatically removed, steadily shrinking the board's effective army and forcing decisive action. To help spectators, use an augmented reality display overlay showing the top two engine-suggested moves in real time with probability percentages, so casual viewers can understand what's at stake each turn. Finally, allow spectators to vote once per game on a \"fan intervention\" \u2014 a neutral event like temporarily blocking one file with a \"barrier\" for three moves \u2014 giving the audience a stake in the outcome and making the experience more interactive and dramatic."
